# Copy to deploy/.env and fill in. This file is read by Docker Compose; never commit the copy.

# --- The instance -------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
# The address people (and GitHub) reach this instance at, with no trailing slash. HTTPS in production.
PUBLIC_URL=https://shelf.example.org
# The PRIVATE port the app listens on, on this server. It is not your site's public port: visitors use your domain on
# 443/80 through your proxy, and the proxy forwards to this port (proxy_pass http://127.0.0.1:8080). Keep 8080 unless
# something else here uses it; never 80 or 443 behind a proxy, the proxy owns those.
WEB_PORT=8080
# Which address the port is published on. Empty means every interface (the original behaviour). When your reverse proxy
# runs on this same server, set 127.0.0.1 so the proxy is the only way in:
# WEB_BIND=127.0.0.1
# Signs the session cookies. Generate one:  openssl rand -hex 32
SESSION_SECRET=

# --- The GitHub App -----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Leave all of these EMPTY on the first start: the web service then offers a one-click registration at PUBLIC_URL and
# shows you exactly what to paste here. Then restart:  docker compose -f deploy/compose.yml --env-file deploy/.env up -d
# If you created the App by hand instead, every value is on the App's settings page (Settings, Developer settings,
# GitHub Apps, Edit):
#   ID            top of the page, "App ID", a number
#   SLUG          the name in the App's public link, github.com/apps/<slug>
#   CLIENT_ID     just under the App ID, starts with Iv (not the App ID)
#   CLIENT_SECRET "Client secrets", Generate a new client secret (shown once)
#   WEBHOOK_SECRET the long random string you typed in the webhook secret field
GITHUB_APP_ID=
GITHUB_APP_SLUG=
GITHUB_APP_CLIENT_ID=
GITHUB_APP_CLIENT_SECRET=
GITHUB_WEBHOOK_SECRET=
# The private key is a FILE, not a line here: put the .pem you downloaded at deploy/secrets/github-app.pem. It is mounted
# read-only into the containers. (The one-click registration page offers it as a download.)

# --- The agents' model --------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Pick ONE way to give the agents a model.
# 1. An Anthropic API key:
ANTHROPIC_API_KEY=
# 2. A Claude subscription token (run `claude setup-token` on a machine where you are logged in):
# CLAUDE_CODE_OAUTH_TOKEN=
# 3. An Anthropic-compatible gateway (LiteLLM, or a local model server) instead of Anthropic:
# ANTHROPIC_BASE_URL=http://gateway:4000
# ANTHROPIC_AUTH_TOKEN=

# Tools for the agents. The worker image carries no JDK, Node or Maven: when a repository declares them (mise.toml or
# .tool-versions) the agent installs them with mise into a cache of that repository's own (a volume), then runs the
# repository's own build and tests. The sandbox may reach GitHub, the tool downloads and the common package registries;
# add your own (a private Maven or npm mirror) here, separated by commas:
# SHELF_ALLOWED_DOMAINS=nexus.example.org,registry.example.org

# CI. After the repository's own commands pass, the checks read the pull request's CI and wait for it (a cancelled or
# timed-out run counts as a failure). One step looks every minute for this long, then looks again later; a commit that
# shows no CI run within the grace is a repository without CI. Seconds:
# SHELF_CI_WAIT=1800
# SHELF_CI_GRACE=120

# Check that the agents work (a shell, the repository's CLAUDE.md, the title convention, the sandbox, the credential):
#   docker compose -f deploy/compose.yml exec worker shelf-doctor
# To use a specific Claude Code executable instead of the one bundled with the SDK:
# SHELF_CLAUDE_CLI=/path/to/claude

# Agents run commands inside Claude Code's sandbox. If `shelf-doctor` says the sandbox cannot start, on an Ubuntu 23.10+ host fix
# the host (the sandbox needs user namespaces, which Ubuntu restricts in two places). One script looks, explains, asks, fixes:
#   sh deploy/fix-ubuntu.sh
# LAST RESORT, only for repositories you fully trust: turn the sandbox off. An agent can then read the worker's environment
# and its mounted secrets, so anything in this file becomes readable by what the agents run.
# SHELF_SANDBOX_DISABLED=1

# Optional: force a model, and bound what one step may spend.
# Warden guards every agent: it checks each command, file access and fetch before it runs, keeps secrets and personal data
# off the model and logs every decision. Profile: shelf (default), strict, or observe (logs only, blocks nothing: a good
# first week). To find out whether Warden is what stops a task, SHELF_WARDEN=off and run install.sh again.
# SHELF_WARDEN_PROFILE=shelf
# SHELF_WARDEN=on
# SHELF_MODEL=
SHELF_MAX_TURNS=40
# SHELF_MAX_BUDGET_USD=2

# --- Temporal -----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Temporal's own web interface is served to signed-in ADMINISTRATORS (listed in shelf.toml) at /temporal on your address, read-only:
# list workflows, read each one's history, run queries. To also allow signals and termination from there, set:
# TEMPORAL_UI_WRITABLE=1
# The bundled Temporal needs nothing. To use your own cluster, set the address (and delete the temporal service):
# TEMPORAL_ADDRESS=temporal.example.org:7233
# TEMPORAL_NAMESPACE=default
